      "content": [
        {
          "text": "4. More Control Flow Tools"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "7814955c744f",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "As well as the "
        },
        {
          "text": "while",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ],
          "link": {
            "href": "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#while",
            "kind": "same_domain"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " statement just introduced, Python uses a few more that we will encounter in this chapter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c203c238e702",
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "anchor": "if-statements",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "4.1. "
        },
        {
          "text": "if",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " Statements"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "436594059a83",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Perhaps the most well-known statement type is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "if",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ],
          "link": {
            "href": "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#if",
            "kind": "same_domain"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " statement. For example:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "56a498047661",
      "kind": "code",
      "lang": "python3",
      "text": ">>> x = int(input(\"Please enter an integer: \"))\nPlease enter an integer: 42\n>>> if x < 0:\n...     x = 0\n...     print('Negative changed to zero')\n... elif x == 0:\n...     print('Zero')\n... elif x == 1:\n...     print('Single')\n... else:\n...     print('More')\n...\nMore\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "1e3f85087af4",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "There can be zero or more "
        },
        {
          "text": "elif",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ],
          "link": {
            "href": "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#elif",
            "kind": "same_domain"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " parts, and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "else",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ],
          "link": {
            "href": "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#else",
            "kind": "same_domain"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " part is optional. The keyword ‘"
        },
        {
          "text": "elif",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "’ is short for ‘else if’, and is useful to avoid excessive indentation. An "
        },
        {
          "text": "if",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " … "
        },
        {
          "text": "elif",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " … "
        },
        {
          "text": "elif",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " … sequence is a substitute for the "
        },
        {
          "text": "switch",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " or "
        },
        {
          "text": "case",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " statements found in other languages."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6cb21ba593a5",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "If you’re comparing the same value to several constants, or checking for specific types or attributes, you may also find the "
        },
        {
          "text": "match",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " statement useful. For more details see "
        },
        {
          "text": "match Statements",
          "link": {
            "href": "#tut-match",
            "kind": "anchor"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "afe4f5c6b585",
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "anchor": "for-statements",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "4.2. "
        },
        {
          "text": "for",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " Statements"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "714ebffae54d",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "The "
        },
        {
          "text": "for",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ],
          "link": {
            "href": "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#for",
            "kind": "same_domain"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " statement in Python differs a bit from what you may be used to in C or Pascal. Rather than always iterating over an arithmetic progression of numbers (like in Pascal), or giving the user the ability to define both the iteration step and halting condition (as C), Python’s "
        },
        {
          "text": "for",
          "marks": [
            "code"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " statement iterates over the items of any sequence (a list or a string), in the order that they appear in the sequence. For example (no pun intended):"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fdcd449f8cdb",
      "kind": "code",
      "lang": "python3",
      "text": ">>> # Measure some strings:\n>>> words = ['cat', 'window', 'defenestrate']\n>>> for w in words:\n...     print(w, len(w))\n...\ncat 3\nwindow 6\ndefenestrate 12\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "ec800b1845bb",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Code that modifies a collection while iterating over that same collection can be tricky to get right. Instead, it is usually more straight-forward to loop over a copy of the collection or to create a new collection:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d5052a25f8ff",
      "kind": "code",
      "lang": "python3",
      "text": "# Create a sample collection\nusers = {'Hans': 'active', 'Éléonore': 'inactive', '景太郎': 'active'}\n\n# Strategy:  Iterate over a copy\nfor user, status in users.copy().items():\n    if status == 'inactive':\n        del users[user]\n\n# Strategy:  Create a new collection\nactive_users = {}\nfor user, status in users.items():\n    if status == 'active':\n        active_users[user] = status\n"
    }
  ]
}
